<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  App-level privacy manifest for Exactly 67. It covers the app's own binary
	  and the Capacitor plugins, which are SPM packages compiled INTO the app
	  target (CapApp-SPM): a package without its own PrivacyInfo resource is
	  covered by this file, so their required-reason API use is declared here.

	  This file used to say "fully offline: collects no data and does not
	  track". That stopped being true when the game started showing ads, first
	  through AdMob and, since Google terminated that account, through Unity
	  LevelPlay. The ad SDK does collect data and does track, gated by consent
	  and the ATT prompt. The declarations below are still right, for a
	  different reason, written out so nobody "fixes" them back:

	  NSPrivacyTracking is FALSE, deliberately, with NO tracking domains.
	  KVIZKO, the sibling app on the same LevelPlay stack, shipped this file
	  with <true/> and an empty domain list, and Apple rejected its first App
	  Store submission (build 62, 2026-09-15) with:

	    ITMS-91064: Invalid tracking information. A PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy file
	    contains invalid tracking information at the following path:
	    "PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy". NSPrivacyTracking must be true if
	    NSPrivacyTrackingDomains isn't empty.

	  The message states the rule one way round; what actually failed is the
	  other: declaring tracking while listing NO domains is an inconsistent
	  declaration, and the validator refuses it. The two keys must agree in
	  BOTH directions.

	  False is the true answer for THIS file. The app's own code and the
	  plugins compiled into it send nothing anywhere; the tracking is done by
	  the ad SDK, and the LevelPlay (IronSource) and Unity Ads frameworks ship
	  their OWN privacy manifests and answer for their own declarations.
	  Nothing else is softened by this: App Privacy in App Store Connect still
	  declares the advertising identifier as used for tracking, the ATT prompt
	  still runs, and NSUserTrackingUsageDescription stays in Info.plist.

	  If tracking is ever declared true here again, the real tracking domains
	  must be listed with it, taken from a list the network publishes. Guessing
	  that list is simply a different false statement.

	  TestFlight does not run this validation. A wrong manifest sails through
	  every TestFlight upload and is rejected only when a build is submitted to
	  the App Store, so a green TestFlight build proves nothing about this file.

	  NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes is empty for the same reason: the app's own
	  code collects nothing. What the ad SDK collects is declared in its own
	  manifest and in App Privacy.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<!--
		  UserDefaults (CA92.1: read and write data only accessible to the app
		  itself). @capacitor/preferences keeps every exactly67.* key there
		  (progress, sound / music / haptics switches, hint inventory, the ad
		  cadence counters, the purchase entitlements), and capacitor-levelplay-ads
		  keeps the consent decision and the IABTCF_* strings there. Both access
		  it from the app's own code, on behalf of the app.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
